Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.170
Hi, everyone.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.520
Let's get started.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.800
Hope the first
homework is going well.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.785
So the topic for today
is arithmetic coding.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.380
OK.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:27.080
OK, so quickly going over the
quiz questions from last time.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:33.670
So we had a question on you have
a binary source with this set,

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:36.730
with this probability
distribution, so Bernoulli 1/2.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:39.820
And we asked, What is the
size of the typical set

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.130
as a function of n?

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:46.115
Anybody wants to
guess the answer?

00:00:51.913 --> 00:00:54.380
2 to the power of n.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.270
Yeah, I'm hearing 2 to the
power n which is correct.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.250
So this is sort
of a special case.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.490
So last time we drew this
diagram where we said--

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.620
so this is like
all the sequences

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.260
and then we said like
there's a much smaller set,

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.830
the typical set which has
the typical sequences.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:13.740
But in this case, the
entropy is actually 1.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.085
So actually, every
sequence is typical.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:26.190
Not every, like almost
every sequence is typical.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:33.470
I guess it depends on your
definition of typicality.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.810
So if you consider
a definition where

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:40.790
if it's typical, if you
have exactly half zeros,

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:43.910
half ones, then not every
sequence is typical clearly.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:45.710
But there are other
definitions of typical

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:52.115
under which typicality under
which every sequence is typical.

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:01.140
Anybody who didn't
get this question?

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.930
I just want to hear
like some other views

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:06.200
on how people approach this just
to make sure people followed.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:13.520
OK.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.803
OK, meet me after
class I guess if you

00:02:15.803 --> 00:02:17.595
thought of a different
way of solving this.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:26.090
OK, so this question was about
KL divergence relative entropy.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:30.870
So here we said we have a
source, Bernoulli 1/2 again.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.410
We said Zoro knows the
distribution of this source,

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:38.510
designs up per-symbol Huffman
code for this sequence.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:45.350
So this code as you
have seen now many times

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:49.120
is just going to be
this very simple code

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.970
because on two symbols
this is the Huffman code.

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:53.980
However, Luffy doesn't
know the distribution

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.650
and encodes it using a
per-symbol code assuming

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:59.530
some other distribution.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:05.110
But you again know that for
binary per-symbol Huffman code

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.930
is always going to be this tree.

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.590
So the question was, How many
extra bits in expectation

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.740
does Luffy need
over Zoro to encode

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:16.630
a sequence from the source, P?

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:21.400
It's going to be 0.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.745
There is no penalty
because it's the same code.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:34.210
Yeah, next question
was we asked you

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:36.910
to tell the KL divergence
between the distributions, P

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:40.060
and Q. And I calculated
this at home.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.990
So this is just this calculation
based on the formula of the KL

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:44.670
divergence.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.770
And this comes out to be 0.2.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:59.990
And the third part
was, OK, so this

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:01.820
is more of the real question.

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:03.770
That in class we learned
that KL divergence,

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:07.280
so last time covered that KL
divergence is an indicator.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.890
Like it tells you how much
excess code length would

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:13.640
you use if your distribution
for which you designed the code

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:16.010
doesn't match the
real distribution

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:17.390
from which the data is coming.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:21.230
But we see here that we are
not paying any penalty here.

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:37.900
Even though the KL divergence
is 0.2075, why is that?

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:41.550
Is an indicator of like how
much you may have of entropy.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:44.260
And since in this case you're--

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:47.110
it was a little bit
easier for 2 pi over--

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:48.417
we got actually--

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:58.380
Yeah, so a suggestion is that
the way it was stated last time

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:02.520
was that if you have an optimal
code for both distributions

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:06.270
using log 1/P bits
or log 1/Q bits then

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:10.470
you compute the penalty
for using the wrong code.

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:19.450
But in this particular case, you
see that this code this does not

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:39.420
achieve the entropy for Q. So if
you had a large block size, then

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.160
actually this would hold.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:44.490
So then you would get--

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:47.640
So oftentimes, many of these
results are for large and

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:49.920
or when you're
approaching optimality.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.570
They may or may
not hold when you

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:56.310
have in the last quiz we saw an
example where you had something

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.950
where Huffman code was
optimal in expectation

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:01.050
but it was not optimal
for a particular instance

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:02.460
of the source.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:07.680
Similarly, a code like this
result can hold in expectation.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.980
It holds on average when
you have optimal codes

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:14.550
but it might not hold if you are
looking at a particular sequence

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.485
or if you do not
have optimal codes.

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:23.260
OK, any questions on the quiz?

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:31.810
OK, so now we'll get started.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:34.930
Almost all of these
slides are from last year

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:38.530
where Kedar made the slides
and taught the lecture,

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:39.925
so I will be using them.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:46.980
So this is a recurring
theme we have been

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:48.480
seeing for a few lectures now.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:50.670
So we'll finally look
at a good solution

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:53.850
which is that symbol
codes are not optimal.

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:56.340
So this particular
example, let's

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:00.225
say you have A and B where
A has probability 0.1,

00:07:00.225 --> 00:07:02.250
B has probability 0.9.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:06.000
And the entropy here you
can calculate is 0.47 bits.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.790
But we saw that a
symbol Huffman code only

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:10.470
gets you one bit per symbol.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:12.760
So clearly, there is a gap.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:15.840
We have seen that now.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.110
Ideally, and we also read
that in an ideal setting

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.780
for a symbol, s,
you want to spend

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:26.490
log 1/P of s bits per
symbol for that symbol.

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:31.260
But the issue happens is that
this is an important point that

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:34.680
you are using symbol codes so
you cannot use fractional bits.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:37.650
So whenever this log
1/Ps is not an integer,

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:40.680
it is just impossible
for you to be optimal.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:44.100
You pay the ceiling of that
or something like that.

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:47.770
So that just poses a
fundamental limit on

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:49.360
if you are using
symbol codes, there

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:52.240
will be some
suboptimality there.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:56.410
So that's why we see this
one bit overhead which

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:57.370
we have been seeing.

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:03.590
We saw that-- we saw this
result a few lectures ago

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:06.920
where you had like the expected
length of Huffman codes

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:10.820
was less than or equal to the
expected length of Shannon

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:13.120
codes.

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.850
And you had the lower
bound which was the entropy

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:21.370
and you had the upper bound
which was entropy plus 1.

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:23.830
So there was this plus 1
factor we have been seeing.

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:32.610
Another thing we saw in the last
lecture was this AEP property.

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:34.880
And what was AEP property?

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:36.630
We saw that in the
quiz question just now.

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:40.320
That you have the universe
of all possible n length

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.700
sequences and then you
have the typical set

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:44.880
which is a much smaller subset.

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:50.490
And what we saw was as you--

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:55.680
as n becomes bigger and bigger,
almost all of the probability

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:59.820
is concentrated in this
small set in the typical set.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:01.680
And within the typical
set, you effectively

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:03.270
have a uniform distribution.

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:07.200
So really, what happens is
like when n becomes bigger,

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:09.450
you have a very small
number of sequences that

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:11.280
comprise the whole probability.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:13.710
And within that small
number of sequences,

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:16.080
it's a uniform distribution.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:17.970
And based on that, we
saw a coding scheme

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:21.510
that we saw worked pretty well.

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:26.550
So really, this is the reason
like if you take a Huffman code

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:29.610
and you keep on increasing
the block size more and more,

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.200
at some scale what
you will start

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.020
seeing is all the
typical sequences start

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:37.140
getting similar code lengths
with the Huffman code.

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:39.000
And all the
non-typical sequences

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:41.790
start getting very long code
lengths with the Huffman code

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:42.930
or Shannon codes.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:45.510
Any of these codes they will
have the same property where

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:49.290
your set is sort of
split into two parts

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.510
to some degree of
approximation where

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:55.140
all the typical sequences are
coded with a fixed length code,

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:57.840
and all the non typical are also
coded with a fixed length code

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:00.990
but with a much longer length.

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:03.180
So that's just intuition we saw.

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:06.940
But that doesn't tell us how
to actually do this practically

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:10.960
and that is the theme
of today's lecture.

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:12.790
OK, so we saw the solution.

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:14.770
One solution was
to use block codes.

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:16.210
And we said we could--

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:18.752
we did some examples
in quiz and in class

00:10:18.752 --> 00:10:20.710
where you have-- you take
two symbols at a time

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:22.840
and then overhead is
one bit per two symbols.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:24.970
So it's like half
bit per symbol.

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:28.870
And here you keep
increasing the block size,

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:30.820
your entropy is 0.47.

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:33.460
And you see how the average
code length it slowly

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:37.360
decreases and actually becomes
very close to the entropy after

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:38.560
at block length 5.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:39.700
So that's good.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.050
And here you draw
some code lengths.

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:50.800
So at block size 1, it's
just the simple code, AB.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:55.720
Block size 2, block size 3,
you can keep making these.

00:11:01.090 --> 00:11:02.560
And these are the
results that we

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:06.340
saw where the expected
length is between X plus 1

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:08.890
and HX for a single
symbol, and when

00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:10.810
you're working with
blocks of size B

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:14.050
then the overhead
becomes 1/B instead of 1.

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:16.555
And this is just a recap still.

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.580
OK, any questions so far?

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:28.080
Are we comfortable with why
block codes will achieve entropy

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:29.670
as you make the
block size bigger

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:32.070
and bigger because your
overhead becomes 1/B?

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:33.810
And as B becomes
bigger, the overhead

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:35.010
becomes smaller and smaller.

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:40.090
OK.

00:11:40.090 --> 00:11:43.787
Yeah, still recap.

00:11:43.787 --> 00:11:45.870
So we see that the convergence
to entropy is good,

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:50.850
1/B is not the worst
convergence rate.

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:53.370
It's not very practical.

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:56.250
We saw last time this
exponential curve.

00:11:56.250 --> 00:12:01.320
As your codebook size increases,
you need more space to store it,

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:03.510
you need more space to
construct the codebook.

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:07.620
So your memory usage increases.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:10.440
It very quickly as you might
have seen in other places,

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:12.450
exponential complexity
is very bad.

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:16.140
At 10 maybe it's manageable,
at 20 it's filling up your RAM.

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:20.240
And at 30 it won't
fit any supercomputer.

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:21.680
It increases very fast.

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:26.345
So really, it's not like
the appropriate way.

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:34.150
And one sort of point is
that the problem really

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:35.950
becomes very acute.

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:41.670
If HX is small,
then what happens

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:46.980
is the 1/B overhead might be
very, very big compared to HX.

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:49.800
And in that case, you really
need a very big block size

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:51.660
to get close to the entropy.

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:55.140
So if your HX is
like let's say 0.01,

00:12:55.140 --> 00:13:01.170
then you need block size around
100 to be within a factor of 2

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:06.821
for like 2x entropy, basically.

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:17.210
So there are situations
where this is just not

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:19.950
going to work out.

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:24.750
So larger codebook, difficult
to handle device memory.

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:27.090
Another aspect that we
maybe haven't talked as much

00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:28.500
is latency.

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:30.930
So now what happens if
you have a big block size?

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:35.330
You cannot start decoding until
you have received B symbols

00:13:35.330 --> 00:13:35.830
at a time.

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:37.770
So you can only encode
B symbols at a time.

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:41.590
You can only decode
B symbols at a time.

00:13:41.590 --> 00:13:45.780
So it's not streaming,
it's not really streaming.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:50.183
It's more blocky which may or
may not be a problem depending

00:13:50.183 --> 00:13:50.850
on your setting.

00:13:54.260 --> 00:13:57.050
OK, maybe let me
write another issue is

00:13:57.050 --> 00:14:02.990
that depending on your
block size, you will have--

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:07.380
you will have these
boundary conditions

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:13.140
where if your if your data is
not a multiple of B, so then

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:16.350
you also need to handle
all those cases where

00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:19.050
you have some data at the
end which is not exactly

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:27.220
a multiple of B.

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:28.180
OK.

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:30.250
OK, so that's it for the recap.

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:31.195
I will stop here.

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:37.450
We will clearly see there
is a need for something

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:39.220
better than block coding.

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:42.400
So let's see what
is the new thing.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:44.550
OK.

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:45.930
So arithmetic coding.

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:49.180
This is the thing
we will learn about.

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:54.240
So after Shannon did
his work in 1948,

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:56.850
there was a very long
time before somebody

00:14:56.850 --> 00:15:01.170
was able to achieve the entropy
in a practical way which

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:02.190
was arithmetic coding.

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:04.860
This was in the 1970s.

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:07.740
Unfortunately, they
patented the algorithm

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:10.830
which led to a further
delay of like 15,

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:15.960
20 years before people actually
started using it widely.

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:19.110
But it's a very nice algorithm.

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:22.620
So let's look at some
of its properties first.

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:28.030
So first property is that
the data, entire data

00:15:28.030 --> 00:15:29.530
is a single block.

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:31.600
You don't need to split
the data into block size,

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:34.120
B. If you give it like
1 million symbols,

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:37.180
you get a block
size of 1 million.

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:41.110
So it's a streaming
algorithm in that sense.

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:44.020
The codeword is
computed on the fly.

00:15:44.020 --> 00:15:48.130
So you don't need to maintain
a huge codebook in your memory.

00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:50.110
It is on the fly.

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:54.850
And it is very efficient in the
sense of you remember that 1/B,

00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:57.370
here you have 2/n.

00:15:57.370 --> 00:16:00.280
But you see that you can
make n very, very large.

00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:03.130
If your data is 1 million,
this is 2/1,000,000,

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:04.840
overhead over entropy.

00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:07.890
It's very efficient.

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:12.970
And obviously, it's bound like
in practice it might actually

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:13.960
be less than the 2/n.

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:21.130
Yep, any questions?

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:30.740
Are you explaining that the
entire data is a single block?

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:32.700
On that one right there.

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:33.210
Yeah.

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:36.793
Yeah, so it's like if you give
it a sequence of length, n,

00:16:36.793 --> 00:16:39.210
if you're using Huffman coding
with block size B, what you

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:44.430
would do is you would split
the n, n/B parts each of which

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:45.870
you will encode separately.

00:16:45.870 --> 00:16:51.510
Here you encode the
entire sequence at once

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:53.370
in a single pass, in a way.

00:16:53.370 --> 00:16:56.550
So it's just like a stream
going in a stream going out.

00:16:56.550 --> 00:17:00.810
There is no notion of
blocking the data in any way.

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:04.560
And I think the best way
to understand this block

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:06.839
size equal to n is that the
overhead of the algorithm

00:17:06.839 --> 00:17:08.640
is in terms of n.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:11.130
So if your data is
like of size 1,000,

00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:13.610
then your overhead is 2/1,000.

00:17:13.610 --> 00:17:16.700
Was that the full problem?

00:17:16.700 --> 00:17:19.010
We will come to that
towards the end of the--

00:17:19.010 --> 00:17:21.240
that's algorithm-specific.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:21.970
Yeah, yeah.

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:31.550
Yeah.

00:17:35.110 --> 00:17:39.790
So basically here this method
has the advantage because we're

00:17:39.790 --> 00:17:42.460
computing on the fly, so
we save all that memory

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:47.800
but it's still because of that
we like in the previous scheme

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:50.320
that for block coding
we could get within 1/n

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:52.030
but now we're within 2/n.

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:54.070
So is that basically
that the trade-off

00:17:54.070 --> 00:17:58.090
like one extra bit for being
able to compute on the fly?

00:17:58.090 --> 00:17:58.780
Yes.

00:17:58.780 --> 00:17:59.920
You can think in that way.

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:02.350
That's a good-- yeah, so
maybe if we make a table,

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:03.820
maybe that will be useful.

00:18:03.820 --> 00:18:08.980
So you have block coding
and you have arithmetic.

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:14.970
I think the real difference
is in the complexity.

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:20.740
So I guess the
upper bound on size

00:18:20.740 --> 00:18:22.960
let's say we use the
same n for both of them.

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:27.240
So here you have HX plus 1/n.

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:29.070
Here you have HX plus 2/n.

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:33.920
And then the second is--

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:36.910
I guess you have only
two rows, complexity.

00:18:36.910 --> 00:18:40.540
Here it's 2/n, so
it's exponential in n.

00:18:40.540 --> 00:18:44.930
Here it's maybe you
can read like big

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:48.400
often so it's linear in n.

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:51.340
So you can just make n so much
larger than you could ever

00:18:51.340 --> 00:18:54.490
do for a block code.

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:55.720
And this is nothing.

00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:59.740
1/n versus 2/n and for a
large n that's nothing.

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:02.037
The extra over it is basically--

00:19:05.020 --> 00:19:06.670
I'm sure some
people were unhappy,

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:08.830
but I think for
the vast majority

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:12.100
of people who are interested
in the practical numbers it's

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:13.430
a very small gap.

00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:13.930
OK.

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:22.070
OK, so the main question,
What is the algorithm?

00:19:22.070 --> 00:19:23.703
So most of the
rest of the lecture

00:19:23.703 --> 00:19:26.120
we'll just understand the
algorithm, the arithmetic coding

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:27.260
algorithm.

00:19:27.260 --> 00:19:29.780
And why is it called
arithmetic coding?

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:32.715
What does it have to
do with arithmetic?

00:19:32.715 --> 00:19:33.215
OK.

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:42.080
So there are two steps
in arithmetic coding.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:46.820
You map your input sequence
to the number line or range

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:48.320
on the number line.

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:51.740
And then you communicate
that range on the number line

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:52.670
to the decoder.

00:19:55.250 --> 00:20:04.145
So let us try to do
a simple example.

00:20:07.190 --> 00:20:10.190
OK, let's do this first.

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:11.525
So I have 0, I have 1.

00:20:14.470 --> 00:20:25.820
I have a range, let's
say 0.35 to 0.55.

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:29.060
Let's say I had to
communicate this--

00:20:29.060 --> 00:20:33.110
OK, I have this range.

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:35.630
Can you tell me one
number within this range

00:20:35.630 --> 00:20:38.210
that you can represent
very succinctly?

00:20:38.210 --> 00:20:40.790
Like let's write a few numbers
that are within this range.

00:20:40.790 --> 00:20:49.370
There is like 0.355555,
and there is like 0.41786.

00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:52.340
So tell me one number
which is very succinctly

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:55.400
described within this range.

00:20:55.400 --> 00:20:59.150
Fewest number of
decimal places, I guess.

00:20:59.150 --> 00:21:00.950
0.5.

00:21:00.950 --> 00:21:02.720
Yes, I hear 0.5.

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:05.600
So 0.5, I hear maybe 0.4.

00:21:05.600 --> 00:21:10.580
There are these very easy to
describe numbers in this range.

00:21:13.480 --> 00:21:17.120
OK, we'll get to why we do this.

00:21:25.250 --> 00:21:26.615
Now, let's do another range.

00:21:30.250 --> 00:21:37.970
0.35672, 0.3568.

00:21:37.970 --> 00:21:40.250
OK, now tell me a
number in this range

00:21:40.250 --> 00:21:43.850
that is any number
in this range that

00:21:43.850 --> 00:21:45.980
has the shortest description.

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:00.500
0.35675.

00:22:00.500 --> 00:22:04.203
OK, so I hear 0.35675.

00:22:04.203 --> 00:22:05.120
It's within the range.

00:22:05.120 --> 00:22:09.230
It has a description
of five decimal places.

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:12.230
And you can convince yourself
that you can't really

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:13.160
do much better.

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:17.360
This is basically-- so the
first intuition you will get

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:34.300
is that shorter intervals
take more bits to describe.

00:22:37.590 --> 00:22:39.030
Do we generally agree with this?

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:40.500
If you have a very
short interval

00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:43.470
and you want to send me a
number in that short interval,

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:45.570
typically your number
will be very long.

00:22:45.570 --> 00:22:48.990
Like lots of decimal
places in that number.

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:52.390
And we'll see more rigorously
how this plays out.

00:22:52.390 --> 00:22:55.935
But OK, for now
let's stick to this.

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:11.020
OK, now let's do this.

00:23:11.020 --> 00:23:16.680
I have an alphabet of size
4 where my probabilities are

00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:30.310
like is 0.1, B is let's say 0.3,
C is let's say 0.5, D is 0.1.

00:23:30.310 --> 00:23:36.580
And I map them to the
number line like this.

00:23:36.580 --> 00:23:39.120
So I say like A will
get a length of 0.1,

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:43.530
B will get a length of 0.3,
C will get a length of 0.5.

00:23:43.530 --> 00:23:47.250
D will get a length of 0.1.

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:49.560
So what I'm going to
do is I take my input,

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:52.050
I map it on the
number line where

00:23:52.050 --> 00:23:53.940
the lengths of each
of those sequences

00:23:53.940 --> 00:23:57.060
is equal to their probability.

00:23:57.060 --> 00:24:00.512
So here the second thing that--
so the first thing we saw

00:24:00.512 --> 00:24:02.970
in the previous slide which
was that shorter intervals take

00:24:02.970 --> 00:24:04.290
more bits to describe.

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:16.090
The second thing I
will do is map sequence

00:24:16.090 --> 00:24:25.420
to interval with length equal
to probability of the sequence.

00:24:28.250 --> 00:24:30.980
So what you are going to do,
you are going to do two steps.

00:24:30.980 --> 00:24:34.760
First, you will map your
input into the number line

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:38.030
such that your length
is proportional to the--

00:24:38.030 --> 00:24:40.338
or equal to the
probability of the thing.

00:24:40.338 --> 00:24:42.380
And then you are going to
describe that interval.

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:45.200
And we already saw
that describing

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:47.780
shorter intervals is harder
describing bigger intervals

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:48.710
is easier.

00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:52.750
So and we'll look at a
very concrete example.

00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:55.600
For now just trying to
build this basic intuition

00:24:55.600 --> 00:24:58.090
that 1 plus 2, the two
things we saw before

00:24:58.090 --> 00:25:10.150
imply that I guess
more probable sequence

00:25:10.150 --> 00:25:24.965
will get a bigger interval which
will have a shorter bit length.

00:25:29.780 --> 00:25:32.180
Is this somewhat clear?

00:25:32.180 --> 00:25:34.310
So there are two steps again.

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:39.590
Map your sequence to a interval,
and then describe that interval.

00:25:39.590 --> 00:25:41.570
And what we are
going to do is more

00:25:41.570 --> 00:25:44.900
probable sequences will get
bigger intervals and hence

00:25:44.900 --> 00:25:48.140
shorter bit length.

00:25:48.140 --> 00:25:49.490
Keep that in mind.

00:25:49.490 --> 00:25:54.050
And let's look at an example
and then I will take questions.

00:26:10.970 --> 00:26:13.430
OK, let's come back
to this in a bit

00:26:13.430 --> 00:26:14.870
after we look at the example.

00:26:14.870 --> 00:26:17.120
This is just like you can
do the number line in binary

00:26:17.120 --> 00:26:19.880
but we won't need
it for the moment.

00:26:19.880 --> 00:26:23.540
OK, very concrete example now.

00:26:23.540 --> 00:26:27.050
We will work with this example
where you have this A, B, C,

00:26:27.050 --> 00:26:29.180
0.3, 0.5, 0.2.

00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:30.980
And you have a
particular input sequence

00:26:30.980 --> 00:26:32.480
that you want to encode.

00:26:32.480 --> 00:26:34.490
Remember that the
arithmetic coding is useful

00:26:34.490 --> 00:26:36.620
because we don't want
to do per-symbol coding.

00:26:36.620 --> 00:26:38.750
We want to encode sequences
at once so we'll just

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:40.310
take this sequence, BACB.

00:26:40.310 --> 00:26:43.840
OK, BACB, OK.

00:26:43.840 --> 00:26:48.130
So OK, I hope you
can see that clearly.

00:26:48.130 --> 00:26:51.670
OK, so first step, this
is where you did just now.

00:26:51.670 --> 00:26:55.750
You take your number
line from 0 to 1.

00:26:55.750 --> 00:27:01.240
You split it where the lengths
are equal to the probabilities.

00:27:01.240 --> 00:27:08.300
So here this thing is equal
to the probability of A.

00:27:08.300 --> 00:27:10.420
This length is equal
to probability of B.

00:27:10.420 --> 00:27:15.070
And since the probability is
sum to 1, you fill up the thing.

00:27:15.070 --> 00:27:19.825
OK, next.

00:27:22.650 --> 00:27:24.300
First symbol you
need to encode is

00:27:24.300 --> 00:27:29.390
B. So therefore you go to the
part of the thing which is B.

00:27:29.390 --> 00:27:32.540
So you go to the
middle part here.

00:27:32.540 --> 00:27:35.000
OK, and now your
interval is this,

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:39.950
0.3 to 0.8 which is the
interval corresponding to B.

00:27:39.950 --> 00:27:41.420
So every step we
are going to make

00:27:41.420 --> 00:27:43.130
this interval shorter
and shorter as you

00:27:43.130 --> 00:27:44.420
will see in a sec.

00:27:49.740 --> 00:27:51.960
OK, and this is how
you exactly do it.

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:56.670
So the idea is
that you maintain--

00:27:56.670 --> 00:27:58.790
so this is your probabilities.

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:04.710
And this is your
cumulative probabilities.

00:28:08.050 --> 00:28:12.960
So the way you can think of it
is that you have this thing.

00:28:12.960 --> 00:28:26.110
So we have A, B, C.
And this is PA, PB, PC.

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:33.140
And you have--
we'll call this CB.

00:28:33.140 --> 00:28:35.090
And we'll call this CC.

00:28:41.270 --> 00:28:45.670
So really, this interval, the
interval corresponding to B

00:28:45.670 --> 00:28:47.005
is what is this?

00:28:47.005 --> 00:28:54.060
This is the cumulative B
starting there to CB plus PB.

00:28:57.180 --> 00:28:58.005
Is that OK?

00:29:00.980 --> 00:29:04.100
I just take a minute,
make sure you understand.

00:29:04.100 --> 00:29:05.150
This is very important.

00:29:05.150 --> 00:29:07.370
If you don't understand
it, stop me here

00:29:07.370 --> 00:29:09.950
because otherwise you will
have more and more issues.

00:29:44.100 --> 00:29:45.750
OK, let's keep going.

00:29:45.750 --> 00:29:49.035
And we'll do an example later.

00:29:54.620 --> 00:29:57.360
Yep, OK.

00:29:57.360 --> 00:30:01.290
Now, this is where the beauty
of the whole thing comes in.

00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:06.860
Because OK, you
made these intervals

00:30:06.860 --> 00:30:08.750
but we want to encode
long sequences.

00:30:08.750 --> 00:30:10.730
We don't want to just
encode one symbol.

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:12.620
So how do you do the sequences?

00:30:12.620 --> 00:30:14.990
So what you do is now you
recursively break down

00:30:14.990 --> 00:30:15.920
this thing.

00:30:15.920 --> 00:30:20.120
So after you see a B, you again
break this smaller interval

00:30:20.120 --> 00:30:21.880
into BC, BB, BA.

00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:23.900
So BA, BC, BB.

00:30:23.900 --> 00:30:26.720
So into three parts again where
they are again proportional

00:30:26.720 --> 00:30:29.890
to the probabilities.

00:30:29.890 --> 00:30:35.630
So this thing is I
guess let's call this--

00:30:35.630 --> 00:30:39.620
if these are A, B, C, then
the ratio of A to B to C

00:30:39.620 --> 00:30:44.390
is 0.3, to 0.5, to 0.2.

00:30:44.390 --> 00:30:49.340
So you again split the B
interval into the same way

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:52.370
as you did in the first step.

00:30:52.370 --> 00:30:55.610
So you are going to
recursively split them.

00:30:55.610 --> 00:30:58.100
And now you see that
your next symbol is BA.

00:30:58.100 --> 00:31:03.440
So now you move into this
second part, 0.3 to 0.45

00:31:03.440 --> 00:31:04.773
which is this smaller interval.

00:31:04.773 --> 00:31:07.190
So every step we are going to
make the smaller and smaller

00:31:07.190 --> 00:31:07.690
interval.

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:15.140
OK, and you can
do the calculation

00:31:15.140 --> 00:31:21.680
based on this basically ratios.

00:31:21.680 --> 00:31:24.170
So let's do the
calculation maybe.

00:31:24.170 --> 00:31:34.100
So the interval corresponding
to B goes from 0.3 to 0.8.

00:31:34.100 --> 00:31:44.450
So now the length of
this thing is 0.5.

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:47.000
So the first part, the
part corresponding to B

00:31:47.000 --> 00:31:55.220
will be 0.5 multiplied by
0.3 which is equal to 0.15.

00:31:55.220 --> 00:32:05.840
So therefore the BA interval
will be 0.3 to 0.3 plus 0.15.

00:32:05.840 --> 00:32:08.960
So just split the interval
corresponding to B

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:12.665
into three portions proportional
to the probabilities of A, B,

00:32:12.665 --> 00:32:22.890
C. Please stop me
if you are confused.

00:32:22.890 --> 00:32:24.270
Otherwise, we will keep moving.

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:33.970
And then the idea is the same.

00:32:33.970 --> 00:32:34.870
You keep doing it.

00:32:34.870 --> 00:32:38.950
You come to-- the
next one you see a C.

00:32:38.950 --> 00:32:43.030
And then you again split
this BA into BAA, BAB, BAC,

00:32:43.030 --> 00:32:45.790
and then you get this
small BAC interval

00:32:45.790 --> 00:32:50.020
with this interval,
0.42 to 0.45.

00:32:50.020 --> 00:32:52.192
And then you--

00:32:52.192 --> 00:32:55.870
I guess the smallest
interval, OK.

00:32:55.870 --> 00:33:01.060
Then you again split it into
three parts, BACA, BACB, BACC

00:33:01.060 --> 00:33:03.190
where again, the
lengths are proportional

00:33:03.190 --> 00:33:07.820
to your probabilities.

00:33:07.820 --> 00:33:09.300
So you get this.

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:16.110
And yep, that's
basically the process.

00:33:16.110 --> 00:33:19.440
This is the process of mapping
a sequence to an interval.

00:33:19.440 --> 00:33:23.040
This is the step one of
arithmetic coding where you're

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:26.940
mapping a long, long sequence
into an interval on the number

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:29.310
line.

00:33:29.310 --> 00:33:31.590
OK, I think what
we'll do is we'll just

00:33:31.590 --> 00:33:35.130
do an example together in
which will make me confident

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:38.970
that everybody understands.

00:33:38.970 --> 00:33:40.920
So these are just
steps written up.

00:33:43.570 --> 00:33:45.160
So after you encode
every step, you

00:33:45.160 --> 00:33:47.230
see the interval is becoming
smaller and smaller.

00:33:47.230 --> 00:33:49.650
So you're slowly
narrowing down your range.

00:33:53.760 --> 00:33:55.410
OK, let's do this
example together.

00:34:06.450 --> 00:34:25.600
So your P is A is 0.2,
B is 0.4, C is 0.4.

00:34:25.600 --> 00:34:28.709
And your sequence is BAAB.

00:34:33.620 --> 00:34:35.030
OK, let's start.

00:34:35.030 --> 00:34:37.670
Step one.

00:34:37.670 --> 00:34:41.179
Anybody, please tell me
how should I split my--

00:34:41.179 --> 00:34:44.332
this is 0 to 1.

00:34:44.332 --> 00:34:46.354
yeah, please help me out.

00:34:46.354 --> 00:34:51.510
It's the, like-- you have A
being 0.2 according to the--

00:34:51.510 --> 00:34:54.060
OK, so let's go slowly.

00:34:54.060 --> 00:34:57.471
A starts at 0 and ends where?

00:34:57.471 --> 00:34:58.720
0.2.

00:34:58.720 --> 00:34:59.230
0.2.

00:34:59.230 --> 00:34:59.730
OK.

00:34:59.730 --> 00:35:02.816
B starts at 0.2 ends where?

00:35:02.816 --> 00:35:03.695
0.6.

00:35:03.695 --> 00:35:06.860
0.6, right?

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:08.730
And yeah, we'll do-- slowly.

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:09.230
OK.

00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:13.740
OK, yeah.

00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:16.500
If you find it very
easy, that's great.

00:35:16.500 --> 00:35:19.530
We won't need to--

00:35:19.530 --> 00:35:22.830
arithmetic coding you will see
will come up again and again.

00:35:22.830 --> 00:35:27.240
It's very deep-- we
are right now seeing it

00:35:27.240 --> 00:35:33.600
for a very special
setting but really, I

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:39.630
guess in lecture 8, 9, 10 you
will see it will just recur.

00:35:39.630 --> 00:35:41.505
Now let's expand it out again.

00:35:44.340 --> 00:35:46.550
So this is 0.2.

00:35:46.550 --> 00:35:47.750
OK, Why did I expand B?

00:35:47.750 --> 00:35:49.370
Because B was my first symbol.

00:35:49.370 --> 00:35:51.020
So that's why I'm
now expanding out B

00:35:51.020 --> 00:35:54.260
because that's my first symbol.

00:35:54.260 --> 00:35:57.320
OK, now can somebody help
me calculate what is the--

00:35:57.320 --> 00:35:59.390
again, How do I divide
into three parts?

00:36:01.930 --> 00:36:03.940
So what is the part for BA?

00:36:03.940 --> 00:36:08.360
Just help me out with the math
and then I will calculate.

00:36:11.900 --> 00:36:13.730
So I guess BA.

00:36:13.730 --> 00:36:17.340
So tell me the length for BA.

00:36:17.340 --> 00:36:22.550
How long should be
the BA portion of it?

00:36:22.550 --> 00:36:27.445
Hint, the B portion is 0.4.

00:36:27.445 --> 00:36:30.240
0.08.

00:36:30.240 --> 00:36:36.960
OK, I hear 0.08 which is correct
because B was 0.4 and A is 0.2.

00:36:36.960 --> 00:36:39.690
So this is 0.08.

00:36:39.690 --> 00:36:48.190
And then BB will be
0.4 times 0.4, so 0.16.

00:36:48.190 --> 00:36:51.960
And then BC will also be 0.16.

00:36:51.960 --> 00:36:54.270
Is that clear?

00:36:54.270 --> 00:36:56.460
We are again dividing it
in the same proportion.

00:36:56.460 --> 00:36:59.600
So let's do it.

00:36:59.600 --> 00:37:05.480
So BA, BB, BC.

00:37:05.480 --> 00:37:10.640
And so this goes from 0.2 to
0.28 because 0.2 plus 0.08.

00:37:10.640 --> 00:37:15.290
And then 0.28 to 0.44.

00:37:15.290 --> 00:37:18.890
OK, now which of these three
I go into to further expand?

00:37:22.082 --> 00:37:24.820
The first one.

00:37:24.820 --> 00:37:25.820
Do I hear the first one?

00:37:25.820 --> 00:37:29.657
How many think it's the BA one?

00:37:29.657 --> 00:37:31.240
OK, some people think
it's the BA one.

00:37:31.240 --> 00:37:33.850
It's the BA one because the
second symbol in my sequence

00:37:33.850 --> 00:37:37.645
is A. So this is the
one I further expand.

00:37:42.570 --> 00:37:44.370
OK, this one let me try to do.

00:37:44.370 --> 00:37:48.610
So we have 0.2
here and 0.28 here.

00:37:48.610 --> 00:37:56.970
So BAA, the length
of BAA is the length

00:37:56.970 --> 00:38:02.610
of BA multiplied by
the probability of A

00:38:02.610 --> 00:38:06.015
because the third
symbol this A. And this

00:38:06.015 --> 00:38:12.480
is 0.08 multiplied by
0.2 which will be 0.016.

00:38:12.480 --> 00:38:14.820
So the calculations as
you see are getting harder

00:38:14.820 --> 00:38:15.780
to do by hand.

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:19.710
We will give up shortly.

00:38:19.710 --> 00:38:24.320
Yeah, so this is 0.216.

00:38:24.320 --> 00:38:27.800
And we only really need BAA,
so let's just expand this out.

00:38:27.800 --> 00:38:31.040
The other ones we don't care
because A is our next symbol.

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:34.925
So maybe let's try to make
that clear with some coloring.

00:38:37.690 --> 00:38:44.696
So this B here
corresponds to this B.

00:38:44.696 --> 00:38:53.520
And this second A here
corresponds to this second A.

00:38:53.520 --> 00:38:57.442
And the third A here
corresponds to this third A.

00:38:57.442 --> 00:38:58.650
So there is a correspondence.

00:38:58.650 --> 00:39:00.030
You just keep going like this.

00:39:05.560 --> 00:39:06.130
OK.

00:39:06.130 --> 00:39:11.280
And Kedar kindly calculated
it for us where--

00:39:22.490 --> 00:39:23.180
sorry.

00:39:23.180 --> 00:39:27.140
This is just cheating because
