00:39:58.610 --> 00:40:00.740
But anyway, I think we--

00:40:00.740 --> 00:40:02.060
I hope we get the idea.

00:40:02.060 --> 00:40:04.400
You just keep
expanding out the--

00:40:04.400 --> 00:40:06.680
as you get each
symbol, you expand out

00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:10.280
a particular sub-interval
in your current interval.

00:40:23.810 --> 00:40:29.400
OK, let's look at a pseudocode
just so that it's very concrete.

00:40:29.400 --> 00:40:31.470
So the encode block
function, the step one

00:40:31.470 --> 00:40:34.950
is to find the interval
corresponding to the input.

00:40:34.950 --> 00:40:38.760
So this is in SCL,
you might guess.

00:40:38.760 --> 00:40:43.560
So initially you start with
low and high as 0 and 1.

00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:46.530
And then you go over every
symbol in your input.

00:40:46.530 --> 00:40:48.210
And you shrink range.

00:40:48.210 --> 00:40:52.080
So shrink range is really the
core function of arithmetic

00:40:52.080 --> 00:40:53.190
encoding.

00:40:53.190 --> 00:40:55.830
And what does
shrinkage range do?

00:40:55.830 --> 00:41:00.360
It starts with the
current range and it

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:05.310
calculates the new
L and the new H.

00:41:05.310 --> 00:41:07.650
It's exactly like
the formula we saw

00:41:07.650 --> 00:41:10.740
before represented in a
slightly different notation.

00:41:10.740 --> 00:41:14.640
But yeah, I encourage you to--

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:22.050
like here, for example, rng
is like the previous range.

00:41:25.700 --> 00:41:32.315
This thing is probability
of the current symbol.

00:41:35.350 --> 00:41:44.680
And the product of
these is the next range.

00:41:44.680 --> 00:41:46.720
And you need to keep that
cumulative probability

00:41:46.720 --> 00:41:48.820
table because you need
to know where you start.

00:41:48.820 --> 00:41:55.470
So if you look at
the example we did,

00:41:55.470 --> 00:42:02.020
like here this 0.2 here is the
cumulative probability thing

00:42:02.020 --> 00:42:04.503
where you need to know--
so basically at every step

00:42:04.503 --> 00:42:05.920
you need to calculate
the low, you

00:42:05.920 --> 00:42:07.720
need to calculate
the length, and then

00:42:07.720 --> 00:42:10.075
high is low plus
length which is high.

00:42:18.760 --> 00:42:20.410
OK, any questions so far?

00:42:20.410 --> 00:42:21.340
This is the encoding.

00:42:21.340 --> 00:42:22.923
And we'll do the
decoding in a minute.

00:42:29.098 --> 00:42:30.640
Actually, maybe
let's do the decoding

00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:33.895
now so that we don't do
like a full round trip pass.

00:42:47.580 --> 00:42:53.250
OK, so let's say we have
this example, 0.3, 0.5, 0.2.

00:42:53.250 --> 00:42:58.890
And you have-- you
encoded BACB and you

00:42:58.890 --> 00:43:14.330
got this range, 0.429 to 0.444.

00:43:14.330 --> 00:43:35.140
0.3, 0.5, 0.2, and your final
interval was 0.429 to 0.444.

00:43:35.140 --> 00:43:37.090
So 0.43 is a number
in this range.

00:43:37.090 --> 00:43:38.080
Do we all agree?

00:43:38.080 --> 00:43:47.870
0.43 is a number in this range.

00:43:47.870 --> 00:43:48.830
So decoding.

00:43:48.830 --> 00:43:50.345
So here we are doing decoding.

00:43:55.680 --> 00:43:58.140
So we again do the
same thing where

00:43:58.140 --> 00:44:01.480
we divide it into three parts.

00:44:01.480 --> 00:44:20.620
So this is 0.8, C. And the
number you received is 0.43.

00:44:20.620 --> 00:44:25.530
OK, can somebody suggest
what is the first symbol?

00:44:25.530 --> 00:44:26.490
So I'm now decoding.

00:44:26.490 --> 00:44:28.500
I don't know what I encoded.

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:31.830
What would be the first symbol
I would decode if I received

00:44:31.830 --> 00:44:37.536
this 0.43 as my encoding?

00:44:37.536 --> 00:44:39.460
A.

00:44:39.460 --> 00:44:43.330
I heard an A. Somebody who
said A, Can you explain why A?

00:44:49.174 --> 00:44:53.372
I guess those who
said B explain why B.

00:44:53.372 --> 00:44:57.700
Well, since it falls between
things like the range of B,

00:44:57.700 --> 00:45:00.520
we know that when we
encoded it the first one,

00:45:00.520 --> 00:45:03.880
we gave extra to get that
range so that we could--

00:45:07.540 --> 00:45:08.040
Yeah.

00:45:08.040 --> 00:45:09.980
So very good.

00:45:09.980 --> 00:45:14.310
So the intuition here
is that as you encoded,

00:45:14.310 --> 00:45:17.590
the range only gets
smaller and smaller.

00:45:17.590 --> 00:45:20.740
So if you are going
to end up with 0.43,

00:45:20.740 --> 00:45:23.050
you better start
with B because if you

00:45:23.050 --> 00:45:26.680
start with something else,
you will never get to 0.43.

00:45:26.680 --> 00:45:29.890
So your range will get
smaller and smaller.

00:45:29.890 --> 00:45:32.650
So it has to be B, it
has to be this thing.

00:45:36.090 --> 00:45:40.180
Now we will again
expand it out as usual.

00:45:45.770 --> 00:46:03.500
So after you do it, I
think it will be 0.39.

00:46:03.500 --> 00:46:05.270
Let's do it carefully.

00:46:05.270 --> 00:46:08.490
So the length of the interval
is 0.5 multiplied by probability

00:46:08.490 --> 00:46:14.890
0.3 so it will be 0.45.

00:46:14.890 --> 00:46:30.783
And then 0.5 times 0.25, so 0.7.

00:46:30.783 --> 00:46:31.950
After you do all that, same.

00:46:31.950 --> 00:46:34.320
So this is sort of identical
between the encoder

00:46:34.320 --> 00:46:35.160
and the decoder.

00:46:35.160 --> 00:46:40.050
OK, now you know your original
number you received was 0.43.

00:46:40.050 --> 00:46:42.990
So can somebody tell me what is
the next symbol I should decode?

00:46:50.152 --> 00:46:51.140
A.

00:46:51.140 --> 00:46:53.930
I hear an A from a couple
of places which is correct.

00:46:53.930 --> 00:46:55.520
Can somebody explain why A?

00:46:58.880 --> 00:47:00.830
It's very simple, very simple.

00:47:00.830 --> 00:47:03.050
0.43 lies in the BA
range, that's why.

00:47:03.050 --> 00:47:04.490
That's it, basically.

00:47:04.490 --> 00:47:07.850
At every step you just see
the number that you received.

00:47:07.850 --> 00:47:08.660
Where does it lie?

00:47:08.660 --> 00:47:11.420
Which range-- which
subinterval does it lie in?

00:47:11.420 --> 00:47:14.360
So it lies in 0.3 to 0.45.

00:47:14.360 --> 00:47:18.200
So you see that this
lies here, so BA.

00:47:18.200 --> 00:47:19.970
So now you have
encoded two symbols.

00:47:19.970 --> 00:47:23.990
You have decoded B,
and you have decoded A.

00:47:23.990 --> 00:47:26.300
And I think you can
see how this goes.

00:47:26.300 --> 00:47:28.520
Every step-- you basically
repeat the same process

00:47:28.520 --> 00:47:29.450
from encoding.

00:47:29.450 --> 00:47:32.600
You split your sub
interval into subintervals.

00:47:32.600 --> 00:47:35.330
The number you received
you see, Where does it

00:47:35.330 --> 00:47:36.890
lie in those subintervals?

00:47:36.890 --> 00:47:41.750
And you decode symbol by symbol.

00:47:41.750 --> 00:47:45.260
OK, so now what have
we seen so far is--

00:47:48.470 --> 00:47:53.750
so we have seen how
you go from sequence

00:47:53.750 --> 00:47:58.870
to interval back to sequence.

00:48:01.580 --> 00:48:03.416
So we have-- yep.

00:48:03.416 --> 00:48:05.850
When do you know when to stop?

00:48:05.850 --> 00:48:08.340
Because you could
just keep going

00:48:08.340 --> 00:48:12.018
and make up a
longer to add into?

00:48:15.360 --> 00:48:20.010
So the question is, How
do you know when to stop?

00:48:20.010 --> 00:48:23.280
Like in this example,
I could have just gone

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:25.050
on and on with my 0.43.

00:48:25.050 --> 00:48:30.600
I could have decoded like a
billion symbols if I wanted to.

00:48:30.600 --> 00:48:32.190
There are a--

00:48:32.190 --> 00:48:35.790
OK, so maybe let's write it
down and clearly answer it.

00:48:39.850 --> 00:48:42.450
So the question is
when to stop decoding.

00:48:46.010 --> 00:48:49.640
So the first method which
is what we will use--

00:48:49.640 --> 00:48:52.100
we use in our
library for example

00:48:52.100 --> 00:49:11.250
is at the start I guess we
prepend the length, basically,

00:49:11.250 --> 00:49:14.500
n, at the start.

00:49:14.500 --> 00:49:17.470
You just store the
length, basically.

00:49:17.470 --> 00:49:18.760
So that is one way.

00:49:18.760 --> 00:49:20.890
There is a second
way which I've seen

00:49:20.890 --> 00:49:32.150
in some places is you create
a special symbol called--

00:49:32.150 --> 00:49:35.880
you can call it like end
of file, for example.

00:49:35.880 --> 00:49:37.220
So end of file.

00:49:42.340 --> 00:49:43.690
Then you need to assign--

00:49:43.690 --> 00:49:45.280
because you created
a new symbol,

00:49:45.280 --> 00:49:47.860
it wasn't in your ABC set,
you created a new symbol

00:49:47.860 --> 00:49:54.380
which is end of file, you need
to assign it some probability

00:49:54.380 --> 00:50:00.620
and basically
encode your sequence

00:50:00.620 --> 00:50:06.250
followed by this special
EOF token, basically.

00:50:06.250 --> 00:50:08.800
So there are two ways
to make sure that you

00:50:08.800 --> 00:50:10.120
can end at the right one.

00:50:10.120 --> 00:50:12.460
One is that you just prepend
the length at the start

00:50:12.460 --> 00:50:14.050
so then the decoder
first reads the length,

00:50:14.050 --> 00:50:15.508
it knows how much
I need to decode,

00:50:15.508 --> 00:50:17.200
it will stop decoding
at the right time,

00:50:17.200 --> 00:50:19.015
or you put this
end of file token.

00:50:21.780 --> 00:50:24.690
If you have done like generative
models, LMS and stuff,

00:50:24.690 --> 00:50:28.230
they also have a token
where, how does your ChatGPT

00:50:28.230 --> 00:50:30.510
know when to stop?

00:50:30.510 --> 00:50:33.420
So sometimes you need
this special tokens

00:50:33.420 --> 00:50:35.655
to know when to stop.

00:50:39.710 --> 00:50:41.510
OK, any other questions?

00:50:45.703 --> 00:50:47.120
I think I went a
bit out of order.

00:50:47.120 --> 00:50:51.620
Some of this might already be
in the slides, but OK, anyway.

00:50:51.620 --> 00:50:54.290
So now there are a
few things left to do.

00:50:54.290 --> 00:50:55.490
This is not enough.

00:50:55.490 --> 00:50:59.000
We need to-- the parts that
are left are we actually

00:50:59.000 --> 00:51:01.490
need to put this into bits.

00:51:01.490 --> 00:51:05.950
And then from bits we need
to go back to an interval.

00:51:05.950 --> 00:51:07.540
And then we go to the sequence.

00:51:07.540 --> 00:51:10.180
So this is the part
that we need to fill in.

00:51:10.180 --> 00:51:11.440
Did you have a question?

00:51:11.440 --> 00:51:12.970
OK, yep.

00:51:12.970 --> 00:51:14.943
So this is one
thing we need to do.

00:51:14.943 --> 00:51:16.360
And the second
thing we need to do

00:51:16.360 --> 00:51:23.830
is calculate the number of
bits you use, basically.

00:51:23.830 --> 00:51:28.390
The proof that the 2/n thing
that we talked about earlier.

00:52:03.640 --> 00:52:05.140
OK, so first thing
we'll do is we'll

00:52:05.140 --> 00:52:07.870
try to understand, What
is the interval length?

00:52:07.870 --> 00:52:10.660
How does the interval
length change as we

00:52:10.660 --> 00:52:12.730
keep on doing the sequence?

00:52:12.730 --> 00:52:14.950
So first observation
which should be very clear

00:52:14.950 --> 00:52:17.680
if you understood the thing
is that every time we encode

00:52:17.680 --> 00:52:19.150
we make the interval smaller.

00:52:19.150 --> 00:52:22.360
So every step is making the
interval smaller and smaller.

00:52:22.360 --> 00:52:26.860
So the question is, What
is the size of the interval

00:52:26.860 --> 00:52:28.360
for a particular sequence?

00:52:28.360 --> 00:52:31.930
Can you write that down
in terms of the sequence

00:52:31.930 --> 00:52:33.880
of its probabilities?

00:52:33.880 --> 00:52:35.410
Any guesses?

00:52:40.180 --> 00:52:43.050
Which is the probability
of that sequence.

00:52:43.050 --> 00:52:45.810
So I hear an answer which is
that it is the probability

00:52:45.810 --> 00:52:47.010
of that sequence.

00:52:47.010 --> 00:52:49.680
Can you elaborate?

00:52:49.680 --> 00:52:53.910
Because when you encode
for the first character--

00:52:53.910 --> 00:52:54.780
Yeah.

00:52:54.780 --> 00:52:58.380
--the length of it will be
the probability of the first

00:52:58.380 --> 00:53:02.610
character multiplied
with this determinant--

00:53:02.610 --> 00:53:03.110
Yep.

00:53:03.110 --> 00:53:03.650
Yep, yep.

00:53:03.650 --> 00:53:06.260
So the right answer
is that yeah, it

00:53:06.260 --> 00:53:09.530
is the probability of the
sequence where we are always

00:53:09.530 --> 00:53:11.210
assuming this IRD model.

00:53:11.210 --> 00:53:14.180
That the sequence, the
probability of the sequence

00:53:14.180 --> 00:53:18.050
is the product of the
probabilities of all the terms.

00:53:18.050 --> 00:53:18.770
And why is that?

00:53:18.770 --> 00:53:23.660
Because step one you take the
interval corresponding to x1,

00:53:23.660 --> 00:53:27.570
so which is like
Px1, you split it.

00:53:27.570 --> 00:53:31.470
And then step two you take the
thing corresponding to Px2,

00:53:31.470 --> 00:53:33.000
and then you split it further.

00:53:33.000 --> 00:53:36.870
So at every step you are
taking the subinterval which

00:53:36.870 --> 00:53:41.100
is proportional to the
probability of that symbol,

00:53:41.100 --> 00:53:43.410
so at every step you are
reducing the interval

00:53:43.410 --> 00:53:45.465
according to the probability
of that sequence.

00:53:50.750 --> 00:53:55.250
Let's look at the example just
so we are very confident here.

00:54:00.950 --> 00:54:04.370
Maybe let's look at this one.

00:54:04.370 --> 00:54:07.940
So here after step
one the length

00:54:07.940 --> 00:54:10.790
is probability of
B which is 0.5.

00:54:10.790 --> 00:54:12.530
You can verify.

00:54:12.530 --> 00:54:15.530
After step two the
length is probability

00:54:15.530 --> 00:54:21.650
of B times probability of
A which is 0.5 times 0.3.

00:54:21.650 --> 00:54:26.820
And here the length will
be like I guess this thing.

00:54:26.820 --> 00:54:32.980
So 0.5 times 0.3 multiplied by
the probability of C, and so on.

00:54:32.980 --> 00:54:36.090
So every step the
length of the interval

00:54:36.090 --> 00:54:38.850
is the previous step
multiplied by the probability

00:54:38.850 --> 00:54:40.200
of the current symbol.

00:54:40.200 --> 00:54:43.160
So it's like every step you
multiplied by the probability

00:54:43.160 --> 00:54:43.785
of that symbol.

00:54:52.600 --> 00:54:54.385
At the very start
I talked about--

00:54:57.190 --> 00:55:00.220
what we wanted was that for
high probability sequences,

00:55:00.220 --> 00:55:01.720
we wanted larger intervals.

00:55:01.720 --> 00:55:04.930
And you see already that we
are getting that property.

00:55:04.930 --> 00:55:07.330
If your sequence has a
high probability, then

00:55:07.330 --> 00:55:11.980
every step you will go into a
big-ish interval, and so overall

00:55:11.980 --> 00:55:13.870
your size of the
interval will be bigger

00:55:13.870 --> 00:55:17.260
at the end of the encoding.

00:55:17.260 --> 00:55:18.610
OK, is that OK?

00:55:22.510 --> 00:55:24.370
OK, right.

00:55:24.370 --> 00:55:27.280
So here just what
we said just now

00:55:27.280 --> 00:55:29.200
that the length
of the interval is

00:55:29.200 --> 00:55:33.410
the probability of the
product of the probabilities

00:55:33.410 --> 00:55:36.420
which is the probability of the
overall sequence under the IRD

00:55:36.420 --> 00:55:36.920
model.

00:55:41.610 --> 00:55:42.110
OK.

00:55:52.370 --> 00:55:54.170
OK, now we come
to the second step

00:55:54.170 --> 00:55:57.530
which is, How do you
communicate the interval?

00:55:57.530 --> 00:55:59.090
Now you made the
interval, But how do

00:55:59.090 --> 00:56:00.800
you communicate the interval?

00:56:00.800 --> 00:56:03.230
And the simplest idea
that comes to mind

00:56:03.230 --> 00:56:08.520
is let's just communicate
a point in that interval.

00:56:08.520 --> 00:56:09.930
And then the decoder--

00:56:09.930 --> 00:56:11.640
we saw the decoding
process already.

00:56:11.640 --> 00:56:13.890
So as long as I communicate
a point in that interval,

00:56:13.890 --> 00:56:17.100
the decoder can do its job.

00:56:17.100 --> 00:56:18.990
And the way we did
it was we found

00:56:18.990 --> 00:56:23.190
like a number which
had a small binary

00:56:23.190 --> 00:56:25.320
or a small decimal
representation.

00:56:25.320 --> 00:56:27.450
But here we will do it
slightly more formally

00:56:27.450 --> 00:56:30.050
but conceptually, it's
really the same thing.

00:56:30.050 --> 00:56:34.500
So in this particular
example, your interval

00:56:34.500 --> 00:56:37.680
was 0.429 to 0.444.

00:56:37.680 --> 00:56:39.210
So what we are going
to do is we are

00:56:39.210 --> 00:56:41.490
going to take the
midpoint of this range.

00:56:41.490 --> 00:56:45.000
So at the end of your encoding,
you did like a few steps

00:56:45.000 --> 00:56:48.750
and then you did like--
you kept on expanding.

00:56:48.750 --> 00:56:55.140
And at the end you ended up
with this thing, 0.429 to 0.444.

00:56:55.140 --> 00:56:58.560
So this is L, Low,
this is H, High.

00:56:58.560 --> 00:57:01.260
And then you are going
to define a Z which

00:57:01.260 --> 00:57:03.348
is L plus H divided by 2.

00:57:03.348 --> 00:57:04.890
So it's the midpoint
of the interval,

00:57:04.890 --> 00:57:07.050
and that is what we are
going to communicate.

00:57:14.400 --> 00:57:15.720
These are the decoding slides.

00:57:15.720 --> 00:57:17.100
I think we already did decoding.

00:57:17.100 --> 00:57:19.260
So let me just skip this.

00:57:19.260 --> 00:57:21.660
But you can look
at these at home.

00:57:21.660 --> 00:57:22.875
It's basically what we saw.

00:57:27.130 --> 00:57:31.090
So this step shows that
during encoding, your interval

00:57:31.090 --> 00:57:33.910
and the decoding, basically
the encoding and the decoding

00:57:33.910 --> 00:57:34.750
are very symmetric.

00:57:34.750 --> 00:57:37.420
At every step you follow
the same sequence of steps

00:57:37.420 --> 00:57:41.860
exactly as encoding,
so decoding.

00:57:47.340 --> 00:57:49.680
I won't go through
the pseudocode now.

00:58:06.790 --> 00:58:11.530
OK, now let's come back to this
primer which is till now we

00:58:11.530 --> 00:58:13.510
have been working with
decimal number line

00:58:13.510 --> 00:58:15.910
because that's just easier
for us to think about.

00:58:15.910 --> 00:58:18.580
But obviously, just like you
know that every number can be

00:58:18.580 --> 00:58:20.680
represented as a binary number.

00:58:20.680 --> 00:58:24.970
Similarly, you can represent any
fraction as a binary fraction.

00:58:24.970 --> 00:58:27.820
So how do binary fractions work?

00:58:27.820 --> 00:58:30.490
So 0.1 in binary
is equal to 1/2.

00:58:30.490 --> 00:58:34.930
0.01 in binary is equal
to 1/2 square, and so on.

00:58:34.930 --> 00:58:39.940
So 0.11 in binary will be
like 1/2 plus 1/2 square.

00:58:39.940 --> 00:58:41.920
So this is like 3/4.

00:58:41.920 --> 00:58:44.950
So this is basically binary.

00:58:44.950 --> 00:58:49.810
You won't need to do it,
really, but good to know.

00:58:49.810 --> 00:58:51.910
You can do it in any base.

00:58:51.910 --> 00:58:58.100
So for example, if we
wanted to do 0.3333,

00:58:58.100 --> 00:59:04.350
this sort of thing, so in
binary it will be like 0.3333

00:59:04.350 --> 00:59:09.060
is between 0.25 and 0.5
so it will start with 0.1.

00:59:09.060 --> 00:59:11.910
I think it will look
something like 0.01,

00:59:11.910 --> 00:59:13.410
some sort of sequence.

00:59:19.710 --> 00:59:23.550
And if you don't
want to do it, you

00:59:23.550 --> 00:59:29.190
can use some utility functions
in Python or in SCL to do it.

00:59:36.295 --> 00:59:37.170
Don't worry about it.

00:59:40.180 --> 00:59:40.825
This is not--

00:59:43.820 --> 00:59:44.960
OK.

00:59:44.960 --> 00:59:45.770
OK, yeah.

00:59:45.770 --> 00:59:47.900
So that was just
to say that when

00:59:47.900 --> 00:59:50.390
you want to describe
Z, what you do

00:59:50.390 --> 00:59:53.690
is you represent Z
as a binary sequence,

00:59:53.690 --> 00:59:58.100
and then you just send
over the entire sequence,

00:59:58.100 --> 01:00:00.650
the binary sequence.

01:00:00.650 --> 01:00:05.540
But as you know, you need
a terminating decimal.

01:00:05.540 --> 01:00:10.340
If you had-- in math,
you study that if you

01:00:10.340 --> 01:00:15.110
write 1/3 in decimal it's
like 0.3333, it never ends.

01:00:15.110 --> 01:00:17.810
That's not something
you can encode so you

01:00:17.810 --> 01:00:19.460
need something which is finite.

01:00:19.460 --> 01:00:22.820
So Z is binary
representation can be long

01:00:22.820 --> 01:00:24.650
and it can have
infinitely many bits.

01:00:24.650 --> 01:00:27.410
So this is not something
in practice you can encode.

01:00:27.410 --> 01:00:35.870
So what we are going to do
is you need a Z hat such

01:00:35.870 --> 01:00:39.200
that your Z hat
is within L and H

01:00:39.200 --> 01:00:42.320
and it has a short
binary representation.

01:00:42.320 --> 01:00:44.600
So we already-- like
intuitively we can do this,

01:00:44.600 --> 01:00:47.630
but we are just trying to
do it in a more formal way.

01:00:47.630 --> 01:00:49.700
Like How do you prove
that you can always

01:00:49.700 --> 01:00:52.940
find a Z hat which has a small
binary representation that

01:00:52.940 --> 01:00:54.350
lies within this range?

01:00:54.350 --> 01:00:58.280
So that is what we are
going on about here.

01:00:58.280 --> 01:01:01.160
Like how do you prove that
given a particular L to H

01:01:01.160 --> 01:01:03.860
you can always find some Z
hat which has a short enough

01:01:03.860 --> 01:01:05.420
binary representation?

01:01:13.900 --> 01:01:15.640
So let's say k is
the number of bits

01:01:15.640 --> 01:01:17.530
you decide to truncate it to.

01:01:17.530 --> 01:01:21.040
So you find that
binary expansion of Z.

01:01:21.040 --> 01:01:23.560
And then you
truncate it to k bits

01:01:23.560 --> 01:01:26.050
and then you send those
k bits on the other side.

01:01:37.090 --> 01:01:41.240
Now you come into some sort of
like tricky business where--

01:01:41.240 --> 01:01:43.880
so what we are saying
here is if Z hat has

01:01:43.880 --> 01:01:46.760
a binary representation
something,

01:01:46.760 --> 01:01:52.640
then not only does Z hat
need to belong within--

01:01:52.640 --> 01:01:57.850
so basically, Z hat needs
to lie in the L to H range.

01:01:57.850 --> 01:02:02.350
But if you extend Z hat with
some arbitrary binary stuff,

01:02:02.350 --> 01:02:08.180
that should also lie in the set
L to H. Otherwise, what happens

01:02:08.180 --> 01:02:10.340
is that there can
be doubts about how

01:02:10.340 --> 01:02:14.110
to exactly decode this.

01:02:14.110 --> 01:02:17.200
I will not go into the
technical details here,

01:02:17.200 --> 01:02:20.410
but because last year we
had a homework question

01:02:20.410 --> 01:02:22.900
and then it was very hard.

01:02:22.900 --> 01:02:26.290
We made a mistake and then
students made mistakes.

01:02:26.290 --> 01:02:28.000
But there are
reasons, basically.

01:02:28.000 --> 01:02:30.100
If you don't do this
correctly, then there

01:02:30.100 --> 01:02:33.340
are cases where decoder will
make the wrong decisions.

01:02:33.340 --> 01:02:39.860
So just basically let me sort
of quickly go through this.

01:02:39.860 --> 01:02:43.390
So the point is that what
you need is that Z hat--

01:02:43.390 --> 01:02:47.200
and this whole interval should
lie within this other interval.

01:02:47.200 --> 01:02:50.840
So you need to find a case
as that Z hat to Z hat plus 2

01:02:50.840 --> 01:02:53.290
to the minus k is
within this thing.

01:02:53.290 --> 01:02:54.520
OK, I will just skip.

01:02:57.390 --> 01:02:59.240
I'll skip.

01:02:59.240 --> 01:03:02.650
OK, this is what you get
at the end of the day.

01:03:02.650 --> 01:03:06.580
That if your length of
your interval is H minus L,

01:03:06.580 --> 01:03:12.250
then you need k which is
this thing, log of 1/H

01:03:12.250 --> 01:03:13.570
minus L plus 1.

01:03:24.720 --> 01:03:28.410
OK, apologies for not
going over it in detail.

01:03:28.410 --> 01:03:31.050
But let us do an
example and let's

01:03:31.050 --> 01:03:33.630
try to build some
intuition, at least.

01:03:33.630 --> 01:03:38.250
So if I have 0.35
to 0.45, let's say.

01:03:38.250 --> 01:03:43.860
So this has length equal to 0.1.

01:03:43.860 --> 01:03:47.610
And you can find a
number which is like 0.4

01:03:47.610 --> 01:03:49.350
which lies in this range.

01:03:49.350 --> 01:03:53.120
Is that-- people agree?

01:03:53.120 --> 01:03:59.330
0.4 lies in this
range, 0.35 to 0.45.

01:03:59.330 --> 01:04:01.110
OK, now let's do this one.

01:04:01.110 --> 01:04:08.210
0.355 to 0.365.

01:04:08.210 --> 01:04:09.710
What is the length
of this thing?

01:04:13.776 --> 01:04:14.647
0.01.

01:04:14.647 --> 01:04:16.170
Yeah, 0.01, right?

01:04:16.170 --> 01:04:20.694
And can you tell me a number
that lies within this range?

01:04:20.694 --> 01:04:21.950
0.36.

01:04:21.950 --> 01:04:24.440
Yeah, 0.36 lies
within this range.

01:04:24.440 --> 01:04:31.060
So 0.36 lies within
0.355 to 0.36.

01:04:31.060 --> 01:04:34.540
Sometimes it's just easier
to look at examples.

01:04:34.540 --> 01:04:35.890
OK, now let's do one more.

01:04:38.660 --> 01:04:41.510
OK, what is the length here?

01:04:41.510 --> 01:04:52.250
0.355 to 0.3565.

01:04:52.250 --> 01:04:54.970
I would say, length isn't.

01:04:55.480 --> 01:04:58.430
Yeah, length is just like 0.001.

01:04:58.430 --> 01:05:06.580
And you can find a number
which is like 0.356 here.

01:05:06.580 --> 01:05:17.610
So the intuition here is that
the number of bits needed

01:05:17.610 --> 01:05:26.550
is roughly the log of
the interval length.

01:05:26.550 --> 01:05:28.657
That is the intuition I
think you will develop.

01:05:28.657 --> 01:05:30.240
I think if you look
at a few examples,

01:05:30.240 --> 01:05:33.930
it becomes very clear that as
your interval goes shorter,

01:05:33.930 --> 01:05:36.390
you need more and more--

01:05:36.390 --> 01:05:39.450
longer and longer decimals
to find within that range.

01:05:39.450 --> 01:05:41.250
But the length of
that decimal is

01:05:41.250 --> 01:05:45.510
roughly logarithmic in the
length of the interval.

01:05:45.510 --> 01:05:49.650
Yeah, like in this example, as I
made my length 10 times smaller,

01:05:49.650 --> 01:05:51.390
I needed one more digit.

01:05:51.390 --> 01:05:53.880
Similarly, if you-- in
the binary case, if you

01:05:53.880 --> 01:05:55.530
make your interval
two times larger,

01:05:55.530 --> 01:05:57.030
you will need one
more bit, and then

01:05:57.030 --> 01:05:58.613
you make it two times
larger again you

01:05:58.613 --> 01:06:02.115
need one more bit so that that's
sort of the intuition here.

01:06:14.070 --> 01:06:15.330
So quick story here.

01:06:15.330 --> 01:06:19.200
So we studied arithmetic coding
when we did our information

01:06:19.200 --> 01:06:22.380
theory classes a long time ago.

01:06:22.380 --> 01:06:23.730
Intuition is very easy.

01:06:23.730 --> 01:06:25.650
Basic intuition is
very, very simple.

01:06:25.650 --> 01:06:28.080
Even if you take that
away, that's enough.

01:06:28.080 --> 01:06:32.188
More probable sequence goes
to longer intervals which can

01:06:32.188 --> 01:06:33.480
be represented with fewer bits.

01:06:33.480 --> 01:06:34.440
That's it.

01:06:34.440 --> 01:06:37.290
And then we wanted to make
this compression library.

01:06:37.290 --> 01:06:39.810
We wanted to implement
it in practice.

01:06:39.810 --> 01:06:43.080
And then you just run into
a million different issues.

01:06:43.080 --> 01:06:46.110
Like issues like this
where it's very tricky,

01:06:46.110 --> 01:06:47.670
complicated to prove.

01:06:47.670 --> 01:06:50.880
And we haven't even come
to even bigger issues.

01:06:50.880 --> 01:06:54.390
So yeah, just be thankful
to the people who

01:06:54.390 --> 01:06:57.630
like implemented it in C for you
and then you can just use it.

01:06:57.630 --> 01:07:01.510
You don't need to worry
about all these details.

01:07:01.510 --> 01:07:03.240
But yeah, yeah.

01:07:03.240 --> 01:07:06.310
It's a bit tricky.

01:07:06.310 --> 01:07:08.560
Like there are parts of it
I cannot claim that I fully

01:07:08.560 --> 01:07:09.520
understand.

01:07:09.520 --> 01:07:11.080
We'll come to those later.

01:07:11.080 --> 01:07:12.580
I think Kedar
implemented it, but I

01:07:12.580 --> 01:07:15.122
think if you ask him today, he
would have forgotten probably.

01:07:15.122 --> 01:07:18.100
So just very, very
complicated stuff.

01:07:18.100 --> 01:07:19.195
Just keeps coming.

01:07:22.450 --> 01:07:27.520
So we saw that the
size of the interval

01:07:27.520 --> 01:07:30.100
was log of the probability.

01:07:30.100 --> 01:07:35.520
And we saw that the
key was this thing.

01:07:35.520 --> 01:07:38.490
You get the plus 2 because
you have the ceiling function.

01:07:38.490 --> 01:07:43.440
And after you basically
use this formula--

01:07:43.440 --> 01:07:46.170
OK, so this is what
you get, basically.

01:07:46.170 --> 01:07:48.180
That the length of
the overall thing

01:07:48.180 --> 01:07:52.920
is less than or equal to log
of the probability plus 2.

01:07:52.920 --> 01:07:57.270
So you are just losing two
bits for the entire sequence.

01:07:57.270 --> 01:07:59.730
So this is the main result
of arithmetic coding.

01:07:59.730 --> 01:08:02.430
That if you have a sequence
of probability, p, then

01:08:02.430 --> 01:08:08.100
you pay log 1/p plus 2 however
long your sequence may be.

01:08:18.470 --> 01:08:24.460
So let's just do super
quick expected code length.

01:08:24.460 --> 01:08:34.850
It's equal to the expectation
of log 1/pxn equal to n times--

01:08:34.850 --> 01:08:37.939
because of the IRD-ness.

01:08:42.380 --> 01:08:43.939
Is less than or equal 2.

01:08:50.788 --> 01:08:52.330
So what you get at
the end of the day

01:08:52.330 --> 01:08:54.160
is that your
expected code length

01:08:54.160 --> 01:08:57.429
is less than or equal to n
times the entropy plus 2.

01:09:00.810 --> 01:09:06.830
So this is the main result here
that with arithmetic coding

01:09:06.830 --> 01:09:11.609
you get your expected
bits per symbol

01:09:11.609 --> 01:09:15.250
between HX and HX plus 2/n.

01:09:15.250 --> 01:09:17.680
And I think somebody
asked at the very like

01:09:17.680 --> 01:09:20.619
towards the start of the thing,
where does the 2 come from?

01:09:25.850 --> 01:09:28.310
And yeah, this is
where the 2 comes from.

01:09:28.310 --> 01:09:32.420
You get one from the ceiling
function, and you have a 1 here.

01:09:32.420 --> 01:09:35.300
So that is where
the 2 comes from.

01:09:35.300 --> 01:09:36.859
I don't know if it's very--

01:09:36.859 --> 01:09:39.725
adds any intuition but
that's how it comes.

01:09:42.890 --> 01:09:45.155
OK, sorry I have
to move a bit fast.

01:09:48.340 --> 01:09:52.180
OK, just to summarize,
given any distribution, P,

01:09:52.180 --> 01:09:58.160
you will achieve basically
within two bits of the entropy.

01:09:58.160 --> 01:10:01.298
Encoding and decoding are
linear time very efficient.

01:10:01.298 --> 01:10:02.840
We saw that only
thing you need to do

01:10:02.840 --> 01:10:04.548
is like you need to
multiply some numbers

01:10:04.548 --> 01:10:07.640
and make those intervals.

01:10:07.640 --> 01:10:09.602
You do not need to
save a large codebook.

01:10:09.602 --> 01:10:11.060
Neither the encoder
nor the decoder

01:10:11.060 --> 01:10:13.070
actually needs to
preconstruct a codebook,

01:10:13.070 --> 01:10:14.210
you just go on the fly.

01:10:14.210 --> 01:10:16.490
Every step you just
need that interval

01:10:16.490 --> 01:10:19.310
and you make it smaller.

01:10:19.310 --> 01:10:27.200
One thing to note is you
don't even need to compute--

01:10:27.200 --> 01:10:36.750
you don't even need to compute
P, sort of XN, sort of things.

01:10:36.750 --> 01:10:38.977
You don't need to compute all--

01:10:38.977 --> 01:10:41.310
if you have done like Huffman
coding and Huffman coding,

01:10:41.310 --> 01:10:42.727
you would first
make a table where

01:10:42.727 --> 01:10:47.490
what is the probability of AAA,
AAB, AAC, BAA sort of thing.

01:10:47.490 --> 01:10:49.050
You need to make a
big table and then

01:10:49.050 --> 01:10:51.210
you can actually start
doing the encoding.

01:10:51.210 --> 01:10:52.250
Here, nothing.

01:10:52.250 --> 01:10:54.750
You just need per
symbol probabilities

01:10:54.750 --> 01:10:57.090
and you just go sequentially.

01:10:59.610 --> 01:11:01.320
And this last point
is going to be

01:11:01.320 --> 01:11:03.120
very crucial in the
next few lectures.

01:11:03.120 --> 01:11:04.500
Don't worry about it for now.

01:11:04.500 --> 01:11:08.010
Is that here we assume that P
was fixed but you can actually

01:11:08.010 --> 01:11:10.750
vary P at every step and it
still works with arithmetic

01:11:10.750 --> 01:11:11.250
coding.

01:11:11.250 --> 01:11:15.400
So it's a very, very
powerful algorithm.

01:11:15.400 --> 01:11:20.540
This is like
lecture 8 next week.

01:11:27.030 --> 01:11:28.830
OK, in the few
minutes we have left

01:11:28.830 --> 01:11:31.560
let's talk about a
couple practical issues.

01:11:31.560 --> 01:11:35.280
So as we keep doing this,
What do you observe?

01:11:35.280 --> 01:11:37.110
Do you think you will
run into some issues

01:11:37.110 --> 01:11:39.930
if you have a 100 length
sequence or a 1,000 length

01:11:39.930 --> 01:11:41.840
sequence?

01:11:41.840 --> 01:11:43.370
What do you see the intervals?

01:11:43.370 --> 01:11:48.326
Are there looking-- what
do you observe, basically?

01:11:48.326 --> 01:11:53.570
You would have precision,
which as you go--

01:11:53.570 --> 01:11:54.070
Yeah.

01:11:54.070 --> 01:11:58.022
Yeah, so one suggestion is that
as you go more and more right,

01:11:58.022 --> 01:12:00.730
the high and the low are getting
closer and closer to each other.

01:12:00.730 --> 01:12:02.563
Very soon you will run
into precision issues

01:12:02.563 --> 01:12:04.060
because on your computer.

01:12:04.060 --> 01:12:08.170
If you have a 64-bit float, it
can only have so much precision.

01:12:08.170 --> 01:12:10.210
And it's very quick.

01:12:10.210 --> 01:12:13.540
Like within like 100 symbols you
would run into precision issues

01:12:13.540 --> 01:12:15.580
where your high and low
cannot be distinguished

01:12:15.580 --> 01:12:17.530
on your hardware.

01:12:17.530 --> 01:12:25.250
So any suggestions
on what you could do?

01:12:29.910 --> 01:12:31.260
Let me not ask for suggestions.

01:12:31.260 --> 01:12:32.460
Let me tell you what you do.

01:12:32.460 --> 01:12:37.780
You basically-- if you reach a
point where L and H have become

01:12:37.780 --> 01:12:41.140
very close, what you do is
you say that, OK, if you have

01:12:41.140 --> 01:12:44.140
any initial digits which
are common between L and H,

01:12:44.140 --> 01:12:45.640
you release those bits.

01:12:45.640 --> 01:12:48.490
You say that OK, these bits,
so what we are saying here

01:12:48.490 --> 01:12:52.820
is let's say you are in
this very small range that's

01:12:52.820 --> 01:13:00.020
from 0.429 to 0.444.

01:13:00.020 --> 01:13:06.570
You already know first
digit is going to be 4.

01:13:06.570 --> 01:13:08.827
Whatever interval you are
going to end up with you

01:13:08.827 --> 01:13:11.040
know the first digit is 4.

01:13:11.040 --> 01:13:12.840
So what you can do
is you can release

01:13:12.840 --> 01:13:15.750
that 4 and you can
make a new thing which

01:13:15.750 --> 01:13:19.980
is going from 0.29 to 0.44.

01:13:19.980 --> 01:13:21.780
This is just like
an illustration.

01:13:21.780 --> 01:13:24.840
So the idea is that you can
keep releasing those bits.

01:13:24.840 --> 01:13:29.100
You don't need to keep L and
H getting closer and closer.

01:13:29.100 --> 01:13:30.930
At some point, if you
see that L and H have

01:13:30.930 --> 01:13:35.040
a lot of common prefix, you
just release the prefix.

01:13:35.040 --> 01:13:37.500
This is the sort of thing
that gets very tricky when you

01:13:37.500 --> 01:13:38.895
actually start to implement it.

01:13:41.530 --> 01:13:44.020
So the idea is that
if L and H both start

01:13:44.020 --> 01:13:48.310
with a common substring, then
any value lying within this

01:13:48.310 --> 01:13:50.260
will also start with this.

01:13:50.260 --> 01:13:54.810
So you output the
bits, basically.

01:13:54.810 --> 01:13:56.080
So it's a streaming algorithm.

01:13:56.080 --> 01:13:58.080
It's not like you need
to wait till the very end

01:13:58.080 --> 01:13:59.730
to output a single bit.

01:13:59.730 --> 01:14:02.650
As you go along, you
keep on rescaling.

01:14:05.280 --> 01:14:06.840
We will not do rescaling now.

01:14:06.840 --> 01:14:09.000
OK, let's keep going.

01:14:14.830 --> 01:14:17.020
If you're interested,
I think some of you

01:14:17.020 --> 01:14:19.450
might be interested in
how it actually works.

01:14:19.450 --> 01:14:22.210
We encourage you to look
at the SCL representation

01:14:22.210 --> 01:14:24.093
and implementation.

01:14:24.093 --> 01:14:26.260
And what you will see is
that the SCL implementation

01:14:26.260 --> 01:14:29.103
has links to some blog
posts which are basically

01:14:29.103 --> 01:14:30.520
the only places
where you can find

01:14:30.520 --> 01:14:33.370
a description of the algorithm
like a readable description.

01:14:33.370 --> 01:14:38.140
So just go to those blog posts
if you want to understand.

01:14:38.140 --> 01:14:42.230
Here we are just trying to give
you like the high level idea.

01:14:42.230 --> 01:14:44.860
And in the assignments
you will do some examples.

01:14:44.860 --> 01:14:46.390
There will be a
final project if you

01:14:46.390 --> 01:14:49.990
want to do some of
this in more detail.

01:14:49.990 --> 01:14:51.435
So there are a
few variants here.

01:14:51.435 --> 01:14:52.810
There is arithmetic
coding, there

01:14:52.810 --> 01:14:54.393
is something called
range coding where

01:14:54.393 --> 01:14:58.600
you work with bytes so it's
more efficient on a computer.

01:14:58.600 --> 01:15:02.980
A few variants for practical--
for the purpose of this class

01:15:02.980 --> 01:15:07.120
all of them are the same,
exactly same intuition,

01:15:07.120 --> 01:15:09.970
very commonly used.

01:15:09.970 --> 01:15:13.100
All these image and
video coders heavily

01:15:13.100 --> 01:15:14.630
rely on arithmetic coding.

01:15:14.630 --> 01:15:17.300
Many of these you will see in
the second half of the class

01:15:17.300 --> 01:15:19.190
in the lossy compression part.

01:15:19.190 --> 01:15:22.670
And many of these
experimentals extremely

01:15:22.670 --> 01:15:25.460
like high compression
ratio compressors

01:15:25.460 --> 01:15:27.260
they rely on arithmetic coding.

01:15:27.260 --> 01:15:30.980
And these we will see
in lecture 9, basically.

01:15:30.980 --> 01:15:39.320
So this is the second
half lecture 9.

01:15:39.320 --> 01:15:41.480
OK, last thing is
that arithmetic coding

01:15:41.480 --> 01:15:42.170
is pretty fast.

01:15:42.170 --> 01:15:45.650
It's linear time but it's
due to all these arithmetic

01:15:45.650 --> 01:15:48.410
operations you are doing with
multiplications and divisions.

01:15:48.410 --> 01:15:52.010
It is actually significantly
slower than Huffman coding.

01:15:52.010 --> 01:15:55.280
So here you see Huffman coding
and arithmetic coding compared.

01:15:55.280 --> 01:15:57.290
You see the compression
is better for arithmetic

01:15:57.290 --> 01:16:00.380
coding because you get a
bigger block size effectively.

01:16:00.380 --> 01:16:02.990
So this is good but this is bad.

01:16:02.990 --> 01:16:05.660
The speeds are not good.

01:16:05.660 --> 01:16:07.340
So depending on the
application, this

01:16:07.340 --> 01:16:10.580
may or may not be a bottleneck.

01:16:10.580 --> 01:16:14.300
So the next lecture,
which Pulkit will take,

01:16:14.300 --> 01:16:16.700
you will see two
very modern codes.

01:16:16.700 --> 01:16:18.740
Arithmetic coding was 1970s.

01:16:18.740 --> 01:16:21.950
These two are like
2010s, basically.

01:16:21.950 --> 01:16:25.490
So in the last 10, 20 years
these have been developed.

01:16:25.490 --> 01:16:28.130
They-- especially
this tANS, this

01:16:28.130 --> 01:16:30.590
is like somewhere between
arithmetic and Huffman

01:16:30.590 --> 01:16:34.030
coding and very
fast decompression.

01:16:34.030 --> 01:16:36.790
Still getting the same roughly
the same compression as

01:16:36.790 --> 01:16:38.230
arithmetic coding.

01:16:38.230 --> 01:16:43.810
So you will see something very
new and novel which is now

01:16:43.810 --> 01:16:47.470
used in basically every codecs
or codec or every compressor

01:16:47.470 --> 01:16:53.080
built after like or something
or after 2015 is probably

01:16:53.080 --> 01:16:57.040
using tANS instead of arithmetic
coding because of the high speed

01:16:57.040 --> 01:16:59.110
it offers.

01:16:59.110 --> 01:17:06.980
OK, before I leave,
just remember this.

01:17:06.980 --> 01:17:09.410
None of it else really
matters as much.

01:17:09.410 --> 01:17:12.380
The main point is more
probable sequences

01:17:12.380 --> 01:17:15.530
lead to bigger intervals which
lead to shorter bit lengths.

01:17:15.530 --> 01:17:16.490
This is the takeaway.

01:17:16.490 --> 01:17:20.780
This is what matters
in the next--

01:17:20.780 --> 01:17:24.170
yeah, this is the intuition
behind arithmetic coding.

01:17:24.170 --> 01:17:27.320
And yeah, feel free
to ask questions.

01:17:27.320 --> 01:17:29.140
Thank you.

